@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex overview

Wordwise annotates @emph{uncommon} words in reading-oriented buffers
with one-line dictionary definitions.  The pieces:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A bundled frequency list of roughly 9,500 common English words, divided
into five tiers, decides which words are common enough to need no
explanation.

@item
A difficulty level from 1 to 5 (@pxref{Difficulty Levels}) controls how
aggressively words are annotated.

@item
Definitions come from @url{https://dict.org} over the DICT protocol,
fetched on one shared asynchronous connection so that typing and
scrolling never wait on the network.

@item
Results are cached, and the cache can persist across Emacs restarts
through @code{savehist} (@pxref{Caching and persistence}).

@item
Annotation is restricted to prose: source blocks, inline code, links,
drawers, and code-like identifiers are skipped, and the mode only
activates in reading-oriented major modes (Org, Markdown, nov, eww by
default).
@end itemize

The name and the interaction pattern are an homage to Amazon Kindle's
Word Wise feature; this package is not affiliated with Amazon.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@cindex installation

Wordwise requires Emacs 27.1 or later and has no third-party
dependencies.

With @code{use-package} and @code{package-vc} (Emacs 29+):

@lisp
(use-package wordwise
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/systemhalted/wordwise.el"
       :rev :newest)
  :bind ("C-c w" . wordwise-mode))
@end lisp

Interactively:

@example
M-x package-vc-install RET https://github.com/systemhalted/wordwise.el RET
@end example

Or manually: clone the repository, add its directory to
@code{load-path}, and @code{(require 'wordwise)}.

Wordwise defines no keybindings of its own; bind @code{wordwise-mode}
to a key of your choosing, as in the @code{use-package} example above.

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

@cindex enabling the mode
@findex wordwise-mode

Open a buffer in one of the allowed major modes --- by default
@code{org-mode}, @code{markdown-mode}, @code{gfm-mode},
@code{nov-mode}, or @code{eww-mode} --- and type @kbd{M-x
wordwise-mode} (or your keybinding).  Uncommon words in the visible
part of the buffer are annotated with a small, subdued hint line above
them.  As you scroll, newly visible text is scanned automatically after
redisplay settles, in every window that shows the buffer.

Cached definitions appear immediately.  Words seen for the first time
are queued, deduplicated, and fetched one at a time in the background;
their hints appear as the answers arrive.

The commands:

@table @kbd
@item M-x wordwise-mode
@findex wordwise-mode
Toggle the hints in the current buffer.  Disabling removes all overlays
and, when no other buffer has the mode active, shuts down the network
connection and timers.

@item M-x wordwise-set-difficulty
@findex wordwise-set-difficulty
Prompt for a difficulty level (1--5), then rescan the visible region
with the new setting.  @xref{Difficulty Levels}.

@item M-x wordwise-ignore-word
@findex wordwise-ignore-word
Add the word at point to @code{wordwise-ignored-words} and remove its
annotation.  The word is saved through Customize, so it stays ignored
in future sessions.

@item M-x wordwise-clear-cache
@findex wordwise-clear-cache
Empty the definition cache, cancel in-flight requests, and re-annotate
the active buffers from scratch.
@end table

@node Difficulty Levels
@chapter Difficulty Levels

@cindex difficulty
@vindex wordwise-difficulty

The user option @code{wordwise-difficulty} (default 3) decides how rare
a word must be to earn an annotation.  The bundled frequency list
assigns each of its ~9,500 words to a tier from 1 (most common) to 5
(least common); words absent from the list entirely are treated as
rarer still.

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .85
@headitem Level @tab Annotates
@item 1 @tab Only words absent from the entire frequency list.
@item 2 @tab Words outside the top ~10,000.
@item 3 @tab Words outside the top ~5,000 (the default).
@item 4 @tab Words outside the top ~3,000.
@item 5 @tab Anything outside the top ~1,000 --- the most annotations.
@end multitable

Change it interactively with @kbd{M-x wordwise-set-difficulty}, which
rescans immediately, or set it in your init file:

@lisp
(setq wordwise-difficulty 4)
@end lisp

@node Customization
@chapter Customization

@cindex customization

All options live in the @code{wordwise} customization group
(@kbd{M-x customize-group RET wordwise}).

@table @code
@item wordwise-difficulty
@vindex wordwise-difficulty
Annotation aggressiveness, 1--5.  @xref{Difficulty Levels}.

@item wordwise-allowed-modes
@vindex wordwise-allowed-modes
Major modes in which the mode may be enabled.  Default:
@code{(org-mode nov-mode markdown-mode gfm-mode eww-mode)}.  Derived
modes count.  Enabling the mode elsewhere prints a message and does
nothing.

@item wordwise-max-hint-length
@vindex wordwise-max-hint-length
Maximum length of a hint line, in characters (default 50).  Longer
definitions are truncated with an ellipsis.

@item wordwise-request-timeout
@vindex wordwise-request-timeout
Seconds to wait for one DICT response before treating the request as
failed (default 5).

@item wordwise-ignored-words
@vindex wordwise-ignored-words
Words never to annotate.  Usually maintained with @kbd{M-x
wordwise-ignore-word}, which saves through Customize; you can also edit
the list directly.
@end table

@cindex face
The hint text uses the face @code{wordwise-hint-face}, which by default
inherits from @code{shadow} at 70% height.  Customize it to change the
hint color or size.

@node How It Works
@chapter How It Works

@menu
* Prose detection::             What gets scanned, what gets skipped.
* The DICT client::             One asynchronous connection.
* Caching and persistence::     At most one lookup per word, ever.
@end menu

@node Prose detection
@section Prose detection

@cindex prose detection
@cindex source blocks

Only words of four or more letters in prose regions are considered.
Wordwise decides ``prose'' by the face at each word's position: text
fontified as Org source or example blocks, Markdown code blocks,
inline code or verbatim, drawers, property lines, links, tags, or
keywords is skipped.  Org quote, verse, and center blocks hold prose
and are annotated like ordinary paragraphs.  Because the
scroll hook can reach text before font-lock has fontified it, Wordwise
fontifies the region first (in buffers that use font-lock) so a source
block never masquerades as prose.

Words that look like code identifiers are also skipped regardless of
face: camelCase, ALL-CAPS, and anything containing an underscore or
slash.

@node The DICT client
@section The DICT client

@cindex DICT protocol
@cindex dict.org

Definitions come from @code{dict.org}, TCP port 2628, over the DICT
protocol.  Wordwise keeps at most one connection open and sends one
@code{DEFINE} request at a time; the first sense of the first returned
definition becomes the hint, truncated to
@code{wordwise-max-hint-length}.

Everything is asynchronous.  A request that exceeds
@code{wordwise-request-timeout} is treated as failed; a connection
failure backs off for 60 seconds before retrying, and word lookups that
failed are retried automatically once the cooldown expires.  Failures
never block redisplay and never error into your editing.

@node Caching and persistence
@section Caching and persistence

@cindex cache
@cindex savehist
@vindex wordwise-cache

Every answer is cached in the hash table @code{wordwise-cache}: both
found definitions and confirmed ``no such word'' responses (DICT status
552).  A word therefore costs at most one network round-trip, ever.
Connection failures and timeouts are @emph{not} cached persistently ---
they are retried after a short cooldown in the same session.

If you enable @code{savehist-mode}, Wordwise registers the cache with
@code{savehist-additional-variables}, so definitions persist across
Emacs restarts in your @file{history} file.  Note that the
registration happens when the wordwise library loads; if you defer
loading (for example with @code{use-package}'s @code{:bind}) and want
the persisted cache preserved even in sessions where you never toggle
the mode, add the registration eagerly in your init file:

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'savehist
  (add-to-list 'savehist-additional-variables 'wordwise-cache))
@end lisp

@kbd{M-x wordwise-clear-cache} flushes everything explicitly.

@node Privacy
@chapter Privacy

@cindex privacy

Every word that Wordwise looks up is sent to @code{dict.org} in plain
text over an unencrypted TCP connection.  The words come from the
buffers you enable the mode in, filtered to uncommon vocabulary.  Do
not enable @code{wordwise-mode} in buffers whose vocabulary you
consider sensitive, and be aware that a network observer could see the
looked-up words.

Nothing else leaves your machine: the frequency list is bundled, and
the cache is stored locally.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@cindex troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item ``Wordwise not available in @var{mode}''
The current major mode is not in @code{wordwise-allowed-modes}.  Add it
if you really want annotations there.

@item No hints appear at all
Check that the words on screen are actually uncommon at your difficulty
(@pxref{Difficulty Levels}) --- at the default level, everything in the
top ~5,000 English words is silently skipped.  Then check the echo area
for ``Wordwise DICT unavailable'': without a network path to dict.org
port 2628, only previously cached words can be annotated.  Wordwise
retries the connection every 60 seconds.

@item Hints appear on code
The skip list is face-based (see @code{wordwise--skip-faces}); a mode
that fontifies code with faces Wordwise does not know about will leak
through.  Report the mode and face, or add the face to the list.

@item Hints are stale or wrong
Definitions are cached indefinitely, including ``no definition found''.
@kbd{M-x wordwise-clear-cache} refetches everything.
@end table

